@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.15.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  A meter that can only read a dead source now has a sibling that may revive it.

  `analysis.meter` refuses to report a silence its two read paths disagree about: when the audio
  accessor returns an empty window while the source is online, it reports the disagreement instead
  of a level, and tells you to run REAPER's own "rebuild peaks" action. That refusal is correct and
  it is unchanged in this release, byte for byte.

  What it could not do is act. `analysis.revive_and_meter` is a SEPARATE tool that may: it revives
  the dead read path and reads again, then reports the meter it got.

  WHY A SEPARATE TOOL AND NOT A FLAG. MCP tool annotations are static per tool. A `revive: true`
  parameter would make `analysis.meter` a tool whose `readOnly: true` annotation is a lie on exactly
  the calls that matter -- and a client that refuses mutating tools by annotation alone would be
  given no way to tell. So `analysis.meter` keeps `readOnly: true` AND keeps its refusal, and the
  permission to mutate lives in a tool your client can decline without reading a single argument.

  It reuses `analysis.meter`'s handler by registry lookup and re-implements nothing, so the two
  tools cannot drift apart in what they measure.

  It does not pretend to restore. `revive.restored` reports `false` with a reason: source
  revalidation is not a snapshotted project field, so the tool declines to claim a repair it cannot
  observe.

  Surface 191 tools / 4 resources / 5 prompts -- the first tool added since 1.12.0.
